    "bottomLine": "The best wood stove for most homes: EPA certified, sized for a typical main floor, takes normal firewood and is approved for mobile homes.",
    "description": "If you want one wood stove that keeps the main floor of an average house warm through a winter outage and takes a real bite out of the heating bill, the Comfort Glow CGWS1500 is where we would start. Its listing gives the three facts that matter most for that job. It is EPA certified, so it is built to burn cleaner and pass the rules that many towns and insurers now expect. It is rated to heat up to 1,200 square feet, which covers a typical open kitchen, dining and living area, the rooms a family actually gathers in when the furnace stops. And it takes 18-inch logs, the length most firewood sellers cut to, so you are not re-sawing every split. The firebox is lined with firebrick inside a plate steel and cast iron body, and the listing states it is approved for mobile homes, which narrows the field a lot for manufactured-home owners. Like every stove here, it makes heat with no electricity at all. The trade-offs are the usual ones: it is heavy, the chimney and hearth pad cost extra, and it is too large for a single small room. Buy the Comfort Glow CGWS750 instead for a cabin or one room, or the CGWS2500 for a big, open house."
