    ],
    "bottomLine": "A straightforward EPA 2020 plate steel stove from a known brand, with a 68,000 BTU rating and easy-to-find replacement parts.",
    "description": "The Vogelzang VG1120E-BL is the pick for buyers who want a plain, well-supported plate steel stove from a brand that has been in the wood heat business for decades. The listing names it EPA 2020 certified, which is the current standard for new wood heaters sold in the US, and rates it at 68,000 BTU. That is a meaningful amount of heat, in the range that suits a medium-sized home rather than a single room. Check the owner's manual for the maker's heated-area figure before you buy, because the listing title gives BTU, not square feet, and BTU alone does not tell you how a stove will behave in your layout. Plate steel is the practical choice here. It heats up faster than cast iron after you light it, which is welcome on a cold morning in an outage, though it also cools faster when the fire burns down. The quiet strength of this stove is the support around it. Vogelzang is part of the US Stove family, and replacement baffle boards, door glass, gaskets and blowers made for Vogelzang stoves are sold widely, which matters for a stove you plan to run for many winters. Choose the Comfort Glow CGWS1500 instead if mobile home approval is a must."
  },
  {
    "id": "comfort-glow-cgws2500",
    "name": "Comfort Glow Wood Burning Stove, Fits 21\" Logs (CGWS2500)",
    "category": "Plate Steel and Cast Iron, EPA Certified, Up to 2,500 Sq Ft, 21\" Logs",
    "badge": "Best for Large Homes",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "pros": [
      "Rated to heat up to 2,500 sq ft, the largest rating in this guide",
      "Takes 21-inch logs, so fewer reloads overnight",
      "EPA certified, as the listing states",
      "Firebrick-lined plate steel and cast iron construction",
      "Listed as approved for mobile homes"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Overkill for small or well-insulated houses, where it would be run too low",
      "Big and heavy: needs a large hearth and careful placement",
      "Costs more up front than the smaller stoves"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The big-house pick: EPA certified, rated for up to 2,500 square feet and able to take 21-inch logs for longer fires.",
    "description": "The Comfort Glow CGWS2500 is the stove to look at if you are trying to heat a large, open house or an older home that loses heat quickly, and you want the wood stove to carry most of the load when the power is out. Its listing rates it for up to 2,500 square feet, the largest figure among our picks, and its firebox takes 21-inch logs. Longer logs mean more wood per load, which usually means fewer trips to the stove and a better chance of finding coals in the morning. It shares the build of its smaller sibling: plate steel and cast iron, a firebrick-lined firebox, EPA certification and, per the listing, approval for mobile homes. The trade-off is the one we warn about throughout this guide. A stove this size is only the right choice when the space really needs it. In a small or tightly insulated house you would end up choking the air down to avoid overheating, and a smouldering stove makes more smoke and builds creosote faster in the chimney. If your heated area is closer to 1,200 square feet, the CGWS1500 is the better buy. If you are heating a big farmhouse or an open-plan home with high ceilings, this is the one."
  },
  {
    "id": "us-stove-us1100e-l",
    "name": "US Stove 1,200 Sq Ft Wood Stove with Legs and Pedestal (US1100E-L)",
    "category": "Steel Wood Stove, Up to 1,200 Sq Ft, Legs or Pedestal",
    "badge": "Best Mid-Size Choice",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "pros": [
      "Rated to heat up to 1,200 sq ft, per the listing",
      "Comes with both legs and a pedestal, so you can choose the look and height",
      "From US Stove, one of the biggest names in US wood heat",
