    "description": "The US Stove US1100E-L is a sensible alternative to our top pick for the same job: keeping roughly 1,200 square feet warm, with or without power. The listing rates it for up to 1,200 square feet and includes both legs and a pedestal, which is more useful than it sounds. Legs leave space under the stove, which some buyers prefer for the look and for cleaning, while a pedestal gives a tidier, more modern stance. Check your manual's clearance and floor protection rules for the option you choose, because they can differ. US Stove is one of the largest wood heat makers in the country, and the spare parts market for its stoves is big: baffles, secondary air tubes, gaskets and blowers made for US Stove models are easy to find. That matters for a stove you will run hard every winter. In US Stove's model naming, the E suffix marks its EPA-certified versions, but the listing title does not spell out the certification, so confirm the EPA label on the listing or the stove before you buy, and check that it meets any local rule. Delivery is by freight, so make sure someone can receive a heavy pallet. Choose the Comfort Glow CGWS1500 instead if you need mobile home approval written on the listing."
  },
  {
    "id": "comfort-glow-cgws750",
    "name": "Comfort Glow Mighty Mini Wood Stove (CGWS750)",
    "category": "Compact Wood Stove, Up to 750 Sq Ft, 11\" Logs, 6\" Flue",
    "badge": "Best Small Wood Stove",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "pros": [
      "Rated to heat up to 750 sq ft, right for a cabin, studio or one big room",
      "Listed burn time of up to 8 hours",
      "Viewing glass so you can watch the fire without opening the door",
      "6-inch flue, per the listing",
      "Listed as mobile home approved (USA)"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Takes only 11-inch logs, so most bought firewood needs cutting down",
      "Too small to heat a whole average house",
      "The listing title does not state EPA certification: confirm it before buying"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "A compact stove for small spaces, with a listed 8-hour burn time, a viewing glass and mobile home approval.",
    "description": "Not every home needs a big stove, and a stove that is too big for the room is one of the most common wood heat mistakes. The Comfort Glow Mighty Mini CGWS750 is the pick for small spaces: a cabin, a studio, a basement den, a tiny house or one large room you want to keep warm when the power is out. Its listing rates it for up to 750 square feet and gives a burn time of up to 8 hours, which is a useful number if you want a stove you can load before bed. It has a viewing glass, a 6-inch flue and, per the listing, approval for mobile homes in the USA, which matters for manufactured-home owners. The catch is the firebox. It takes 11-inch logs, so the standard 16 to 18 inch splits most firewood sellers deliver will need cutting in half. Some owners treat that as a weekend chore; others find it a dealbreaker. The listing title also does not state EPA certification, unlike Comfort Glow's larger stoves, so check the certification label on the listing or the stove before you buy, and check local rules. Buy the CGWS1500 instead if you are heating a whole main floor, or the US Stove TH-100 if you want a stove sold specifically for tiny homes."
  },
  {
    "id": "us-stove-us1261e",
    "name": "US Stove 900 Sq Ft Cast Iron Log Wood Stove (US1261E)",
    "category": "Cast Iron Log Stove, Up to 900 Sq Ft",
    "badge": "Best Cast Iron Stove",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "pros": [
      "Cast iron body holds heat after the fire burns down",
      "Rated to heat up to 900 sq ft, per the listing",
      "Classic log stove look that suits cabins and older homes",
      "Flat top can warm a kettle or pot during an outage",
      "From US Stove, with replacement parts widely sold"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Cast iron takes longer to heat up after lighting",
      "Ships by freight",
      "The listing title does not repeat the certification: confirm the EPA label before buying"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "A traditional cast iron log stove from US Stove for spaces up to about 900 square feet, with heat that lingers after the fire dies down.",
    "description": "The US Stove US1261E is the pick for buyers who want the traditional cast iron log stove look, and the way cast iron heats. Its listing rates it for up to 900 square feet, which suits a cabin, a smaller ranch house or a main room with a couple of rooms off it. Cast iron behaves differently from plate steel. It takes longer to warm up after you light the fire, but it stores that heat and keeps giving it back after the flames die down, which many owners like on long outage nights. A flat iron top can also keep a kettle or a pot of soup warm when the electric range is dead; follow the manual on what it allows. US Stove is one of the largest wood stove makers in the country and parts for its stoves are easy to find. As with the US1100E-L, the E in the model number marks US Stove's EPA-certified line, but the listing title does not spell it out, so confirm the certification label before you buy and check your local burn rules. Delivery is by freight. If you want quicker heat after lighting, a plate steel stove such as the Vogelzang VG1120E-BL suits you better. If you have a bigger house, step up to a 1,200 square foot stove."
  },
  {
    "id": "us-stove-th-100",
    "name": "US Stove TH-100 Tiny Home 750 Sq Ft Wood Stove",
    "category": "Compact Wood Stove, Up to 750 Sq Ft, Tiny Homes",
    "badge": "Best for Tiny Homes",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "pros": [
      "Built and sold specifically for tiny homes, per the listing",
      "Rated to heat up to 750 sq ft",
      "Small footprint for tight floor plans",
      "From US Stove, with a large parts and support network",
      "Makes heat with no electricity, ideal for off-grid living"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Small firebox means short logs and more frequent reloading",
      "Ships by freight despite its size",
      "Tiny spaces overheat easily: you have to run it carefully"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "A compact US Stove model made for tiny homes and small cabins up to about 750 square feet.",
    "description": "The US Stove TH-100 is built for one specific buyer: someone heating a tiny home, a small cabin, a converted shed or an off-grid space where floor area is precious and electricity may not be available at all. The listing names it a tiny home stove and rates it for up to 750 square feet, which is more than most tiny homes need, so the real skill is running it gently. In a very small space, a wood stove can overheat the room quickly, and the temptation is to shut the air down hard, which makes it smoulder and coat the flue with creosote. Burn smaller, hotter fires instead, and follow the manual for the air control. The upside is a stove that fits a tight layout and makes steady heat with no power, which is exactly what off-grid owners want. It comes from US Stove, so parts and support are easy to find. Clearances matter even more in a tiny home, because walls and furniture are never far away. Read the clearance and floor protection figures in the manual before you choose a spot, and fit a CO alarm. Choose the Comfort Glow CGWS750 instead if mobile home approval on the listing matters to you, or a larger stove if you are heating a full house."
  }
