        <div id="power-outage" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Does a Wood Stove Work Without Power? Yes, and That Is the Point
          </h2>

          <p>
            A wood stove is one of the few home heaters that keeps working when the grid goes down. It needs no outlet, no thermostat and no blower to make heat. You light a fire, the steel or cast iron body gets hot, and it warms the room by radiation and natural convection. That is why wood stoves are back on so many shopping lists: the furnace stops the moment the power fails, even a gas furnace, because its blower and controls run on electricity.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">No Blower Needed to Make Heat</h3>

          <p>
            Every stove in this guide heats the room on its own. A stove is placed in the main living area, the warm air rises off its top, and cooler air flows in at floor level to replace it. You will feel the heat most in the room the stove sits in and less in the rooms beyond it, so plan to gather the household near it in a long outage.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What a Blower Adds, and What You Lose in an Outage</h3>

          <p>
            Many stoves accept an optional electric blower that pushes warm air out further. It is useful when the power is on, but it stops in an outage. That is fine: the stove keeps heating without it. Just do not choose a stove on the assumption that the blower will move heat to the far bedrooms during a storm.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Cooking and Warm Water When the Power Is Out</h3>

          <p>
            A flat stovetop can warm a kettle or a pot of soup when the electric range is dead. Cast iron log stoves are especially good at this. Check the manual for what your model allows, and never leave a pot unattended on a hot stove.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Plan Wood Like Fuel</h3>

          <p>
            A stove is only as reliable as the dry wood you have stacked. Before winter, keep enough seasoned firewood under cover to cover several days of heavy use, and store some near the door so you are not digging through snow. If a stove is not practical for your home, our guide to <a href="/best-indoor-heater-for-power-outage/">the best indoor heaters for power outages</a> covers portable options.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="epa" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Is Your Wood Stove EPA Certified? Buy Only 2020-Certified Models
          </h2>

          <p>
            The single most important label on a new wood stove is its EPA certification. Since May 2020, new wood heaters sold in the US have had to meet the EPA's stricter Step 2 emission standard. Certified stoves burn their smoke more completely, which helps you, your neighbours and your chimney.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What the 2020 Standard Means</h3>

          <p>
            A 2020-certified stove has been tested by an accredited lab and carries a permanent label showing its certification. The current limit is 2.0 grams of smoke per hour in the crib-wood test, far cleaner than the old box stoves many people grew up with. On Amazon, look for "EPA certified" or "EPA 2020" in the listing, and confirm it on the stove's label when it arrives. Where a listing title does not say it, we flag that in the product notes.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Why It Matters to You: Less Smoke, Less Creosote, Less Wood</h3>

          <p>
            Cleaner burning is not just a rule to satisfy. A stove that burns its smoke instead of sending it up the flue gets more heat from each log, so you split, stack and carry less wood. It also leaves less creosote in the chimney, the tar-like deposit that causes chimney fires. Many insurers, towns and lenders ask whether a stove is certified, so an uncertified stove can be a problem long after you buy it.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Mobile Home Approval Is a Separate Listing</h3>

          <p>
            If you live in a manufactured home, EPA certification is not enough. The stove must also be listed and approved for mobile homes, which usually means an outside combustion air kit and the stove bolted to the floor. Several of our picks state mobile home approval in their listings; follow the manual exactly when you install one.
          </p>

