        <div id="size" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. What Size Wood Stove Do You Need? Match It to the Space, Not the Biggest Number
          </h2>

          <p>
            Square-foot ratings are a starting point, not a promise. Makers rate stoves under good conditions, and your house may be draftier, colder or more divided into small rooms than the rating assumes. Start with the area you actually want to heat, usually the main living space, then adjust for insulation and climate.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Under 800 Square Feet: Compact Stoves</h3>

          <p>
            For a cabin, a tiny home, a studio or one large room, a compact stove such as the Comfort Glow CGWS750 or the US Stove TH-100 is the right size. The trade-off is a small firebox that takes shorter logs and needs reloading more often.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">About 900 to 1,200 Square Feet: The Sweet Spot</h3>

          <p>
            Most families heating a main floor during an outage land here. The Comfort Glow CGWS1500, the US Stove US1100E-L and the cast iron US1261E all sit in this range. These stoves take standard firewood lengths or close to it and are large enough to hold a fire for a good stretch.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">2,000 Square Feet and Up</h3>

          <p>
            Large, open or older homes that lose heat quickly need a bigger firebox, like the Comfort Glow CGWS2500. Even then, a single stove will heat the rooms nearest it best. Closed doors, hallways and upstairs bedrooms will stay cooler.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Why Oversizing Backfires</h3>

          <p>
            A stove that is too large for the space forces you to run it with the air turned down to keep the room bearable. A starved fire smoulders, makes more smoke and coats the chimney with creosote. If you are between two sizes and your house is reasonably well insulated, the smaller stove is usually the better buy.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="install" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Can You Install a Wood Stove Yourself? Get It Permitted and Inspected
          </h2>

          <p>
            Buying the stove is the easy part. Installing it safely is where most of the real cost, and most of the real risk, sits. Wood stove fires usually start at the installation, not the stove: a connector pipe too close to a wall, a hearth pad that is too small, or a chimney that was never meant for wood.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Clearances to Combustibles</h3>

          <p>
            Every certified stove has a label and a manual listing the minimum distances from the stove and its connector pipe to walls, furniture, trim and anything else that can burn. Follow those numbers exactly. As a general rule in the NFPA 211 standard, an unlisted stove needs 36 inches of clearance and single-wall connector pipe needs 18 inches; listed stoves can be closer only as their labels allow. Heat shields can reduce clearances only when the manual permits them.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Floor Protection</h3>

          <p>
            Unless the manual says otherwise, the stove needs a non-combustible hearth pad under it and extending in front of the loading door to catch embers. The manual gives the size and type. A pedestal and legs can have different floor protection rules, so check the one you choose.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Chimney and Flue: The Part That Decides Safety</h3>

          <p>
            A wood stove needs a chimney rated for solid fuel: a lined masonry chimney in good condition or a factory-built class A chimney. Never vent into a chimney shared with a furnace or a gas appliance unless the manual and code allow it. A common rule of thumb is that the chimney top must be at least 3 feet above where it passes through the roof and 2 feet higher than anything within 10 feet. Match the flue size to the stove's outlet.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Permits, Insurance and a Professional Installer</h3>

          <p>
            Most towns require a permit and an inspection for a wood stove, and your home insurer will want to know about it. We strongly recommend a certified professional installer. They know local code, size the chimney correctly and leave you with paperwork your insurer will accept.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="co-safety" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Wood Stove Safety: CO Alarms, Smoke Alarms and an Annual Sweep
          </h2>

          <p>
            A properly installed wood stove sends its smoke and carbon monoxide up the chimney. But a cracked gasket, a blocked flue, a downdraft or an open loading door can push combustion gases into the room. The safety habits below are what make wood heat safe to live with.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Put a CO Alarm on Every Level</h3>

          <p>
            Carbon monoxide has no smell or colour. Fit a carbon monoxide alarm on every level of the home and outside each sleeping area, and choose one with battery backup so it keeps working in an outage. Our guide to <a href="/best-carbon-monoxide-detector/">the best carbon monoxide detectors</a> covers plug-in, battery and combination smoke and CO alarms. Test them monthly.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Creosote and Chimney Fires</h3>

          <p>
            Creosote builds up inside the chimney when wood burns cool or wet. Too much of it can ignite and cause a chimney fire. Have the chimney inspected every year, and swept when it needs it. Burn dry wood, and give each new load a hot fire for a few minutes before turning the air down.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Ash Disposal</h3>

          <p>
            Ashes can hold live coals for days. Shovel them into a metal container with a tight lid, set it on a non-combustible surface well away from the house, decks and woodpiles, and never put ashes in a paper bag, a plastic bin or a garbage can.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Kids, Pets and Smoke Alarms</h3>

          <p>
            A stove's surface is hot enough to burn on contact. Use a hearth gate around it if you have small children or pets, keep a fire extinguisher nearby, and make sure working smoke alarms are installed on every level.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="bills" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Will a Wood Stove Cut Your Heating Bill? Yes, If Your Wood Is Cheap
          </h2>

          <p>
            A wood stove can lower your heating bill, but the savings depend on what your wood costs, what your current heat costs and how much of the house the stove can cover. Be honest about each before you buy.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Where the Savings Come From</h3>

          <p>
            The biggest savings come when you have access to free or cheap firewood, from your own land, storm cleanup or a local supplier, and when you are replacing expensive heat such as propane, heating oil or electric resistance heat. A stove in the main living area can carry much of the load while the furnace keeps the rest of the house above freezing.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Where the Savings Disappear</h3>

          <p>
            If you buy all your wood by the cord at premium prices, burn it wet, or install a stove that is too large and runs smouldering, the savings shrink fast. Add the upfront cost of the stove, chimney, hearth and installation, and the payback period can be several winters. Factor in your own time too: splitting, stacking and hauling wood is real work.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Zone Heating the Room You Use</h3>

          <p>
            The smartest way to save is zone heating. Keep the stove room warm where the family spends its time, lower the thermostat for the rest of the house, and close off rooms you do not need. If you only want the look of a fire in a room that already has heat, <a href="/best-electric-fireplace/">an electric fireplace</a> costs far less to install, though it will not work in an outage.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="wood-vs-pellet" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Wood vs Pellet Stove: Wood Wins When the Power Goes Out
          </h2>

          <p>
            Wood and pellet stoves look similar, but they solve different problems. If your priority is heat during a power outage, choose wood. If your priority is convenience and steady, thermostat-controlled heat, a pellet stove deserves a look.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Pellet Stoves Need Electricity</h3>

          <p>
            A pellet stove uses an electric auger to feed pellets into the fire and electric fans to supply air and move heat. When the power goes out, most pellet stoves shut down unless you have a battery backup or a generator. A wood stove keeps heating with no power at all.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Pellet Stoves Win on Convenience</h3>

          <p>
            Pellets come in bags, burn very cleanly and feed themselves from a hopper, so one load can last a day or more. Many pellet stoves have thermostats and can even vent through a wall rather than a full chimney. You trade outage resilience for less work and more control.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Which One to Buy</h3>

          <p>
            Buy a wood stove if you have access to firewood, live where outages are common or want heat without power. Buy a pellet stove if you want a steady, low-effort heat source and have reliable power or a backup plan. Our guide to <a href="/best-pellet-stove/">the best pellet stoves</a> covers that side in detail.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="firewood" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. What Firewood Should You Burn? Dry Hardwood Under 20% Moisture
          </h2>

          <p>
            The wood you burn matters as much as the stove. Even the cleanest EPA-certified stove will smoke, struggle and coat the chimney with creosote if you feed it wet wood.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Season It Properly</h3>

          <p>
            Freshly cut wood is full of water. Split it, stack it off the ground with the ends exposed to the air, cover the top, and let it dry for at least six months, and often a year or more for dense hardwoods such as oak. Seasoned wood is lighter, has cracks in the ends and sounds hollow when two pieces knock together.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Use a Moisture Meter</h3>

          <p>
            A cheap moisture meter takes the guessing out of it. Split a piece and test the fresh face: under 20% moisture is the target. Buying firewood? Test a few pieces before you pay. Wood sold as seasoned is often not.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Hardwood vs Softwood</h3>

          <p>
            Dense hardwoods such as oak, maple, ash and hickory give longer, steadier fires. Dry softwoods like pine light easily and burn hot, which is useful for kindling and quick heat. Both are fine when dry; wet wood is the problem, not the species.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Never Burn Trash, Painted or Treated Wood</h3>

          <p>
            Never burn trash, cardboard in bulk, plastic, painted or pressure-treated wood, plywood or particle board. They release toxic fumes, can damage the stove and void its warranty. Also never use gasoline or lighter fluid to start a fire.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="types" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. Plate Steel vs Cast Iron Wood Stove: Steel Heats Faster, Iron Holds Heat
          </h2>

          <p>
            Most wood stoves are made of plate steel, cast iron or a mix. Both make good stoves, and certification and size matter more than material. But they do behave differently.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Plate Steel: Quick Heat and Simple Repairs</h3>

          <p>
            Plate steel stoves, like the Vogelzang VG1120E-BL, warm up quickly after lighting and are generally simpler and more robust, with welded seams instead of bolted panels. They cool faster when the fire dies down, so the room temperature follows the fire more closely.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Cast Iron: Slow, Lingering Warmth</h3>

          <p>
            Cast iron stoves, like the US Stove US1261E, take longer to warm up but hold their heat after the fire burns down, giving a gentler, longer warmth. Their bolted panels and cement seams can need resealing over the years.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Mixed Construction</h3>

          <p>
            Comfort Glow's CGWS1500 and CGWS2500 combine plate steel with cast iron parts and a firebrick lining. Firebrick protects the firebox and helps the stove hold heat, and individual bricks are easy to replace when they crack.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Wood Stove vs Kerosene Heater for Outages</h3>

          <p>
            A kerosene heater is portable and needs no chimney, which makes it a quicker outage fix for renters. It brings its own rules on ventilation and fuel; see our guide to <a href="/best-kerosene-heater/">the best kerosene heaters</a>. A wood stove is the permanent answer for owners.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying a Stove Too Big for the Space</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A bigger stove does not mean a warmer house. An oversized stove has to be run with the air choked down, which makes it smoulder, smoke and coat the chimney with creosote. Size the stove to the area you actually want to heat, and pick the smaller size if you are in between.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Skipping the EPA Label</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Uncertified and secondhand pre-2020 stoves burn dirtier, use more wood and can cause trouble with insurers and local rules. Buy a stove whose listing and label state EPA certification, and confirm it when the stove arrives, especially for listings that do not spell it out in the title.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Cutting Corners on the Chimney</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Most wood stove fires start at the installation. Venting into an unlined or shared chimney, using the wrong pipe or ignoring clearances to combustibles puts the house at risk. Use a chimney rated for solid fuel, get a permit and have a certified installer do the work.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Burning Wet or Unseasoned Wood</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Wet wood wastes heat boiling off water, smokes, blackens the glass and builds creosote quickly. Season split wood for at least six months, test it with a moisture meter and burn only wood under 20% moisture. Never burn trash, painted or treated wood.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Heating With Wood and No CO Alarm</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A wood stove is a combustion appliance and can leak carbon monoxide if something goes wrong. Fit a battery-backed CO alarm on every level and near bedrooms, keep working smoke alarms, and have the chimney inspected every year. These cheap steps protect everyone in the house.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

