        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            12. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best wood stove?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For most homes, the Comfort Glow CGWS1500 is the best wood stove: its listing states EPA certification, a rating of up to 1,200 square feet, 18-inch logs and mobile home approval. Choose the Comfort Glow CGWS2500 for large homes, the Vogelzang VG1120E-BL for a well-supported plate steel stove, and the Comfort Glow CGWS750 or US Stove TH-100 for small spaces and tiny homes.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does a wood stove work during a power outage?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes. A wood stove needs no electricity to make heat. The fire heats the stove body, which warms the room by radiation and natural convection. An optional electric blower stops when the power goes out, but the stove keeps heating. That makes a wood stove one of the most reliable ways to keep a home warm in a long outage.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do I need a permit to install a wood stove?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                In most places, yes. Many towns and counties require a building permit and an inspection for a new wood stove and chimney, and your home insurer will usually want to know about it. A certified installer will know local code. Installing without a permit can cause problems with insurance claims and when you sell the house.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How far does a wood stove need to be from the wall?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Follow the clearances on the stove's label and in its manual, which vary by model and by whether you use heat shields. As a general rule, the NFPA 211 standard calls for 36 inches from combustibles for unlisted stoves and 18 inches for single-wall connector pipe. Listed stoves can sit closer only as their labels allow.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do I need a carbon monoxide detector with a wood stove?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes. A cracked gasket, blocked chimney or downdraft can push carbon monoxide into the room, and you cannot smell it. Fit a CO alarm on every level of the home and outside sleeping areas, ideally with battery backup so it keeps working in an outage. Test it monthly and replace it at the end of its rated life.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a wood stove cheaper than a pellet stove?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Up front, prices overlap, but a wood stove can cost more to install if you need a full new chimney, while many pellet stoves vent through a wall. Running costs depend on fuel: free or cheap firewood makes a wood stove much cheaper to run, while bagged pellets cost more but need less work. Only a wood stove heats without electricity.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you put a wood stove in a mobile home?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Only a stove that is specifically listed and approved for mobile homes, installed exactly as the manual says. That usually means an outside combustion air kit, the stove bolted to the floor and a listed chimney system. The Comfort Glow CGWS1500, CGWS2500 and CGWS750 listings state mobile home approval. Check local rules and your insurer too.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

